 The descriptor is a separate physical metadata-pool frame. It has no routed MetadataPageId, is outside the twelve logical bootstrap roots, and is not State/radix. Recovery, read-only discovery, reachability, old-pin protection and conservative directory cloning visit it explicitly. Local evacuation encountering this extra physical frame keeps the existing conservative fallback. Relocation replaces physical generation/LSN while retaining semantic generation. Clearing its root reclaims through existing pin/reachability rules, without a retirement sidecar. Both builds carry the eighth fence member (zero for default files), with distinct 31/32 format tokens. Changes cannot masquerade as ID-only reservation or blind adjacency rebase. Publication retains data fence → inactive A/B → superblock fence. Full costs must count the actual 4 KiB descriptor frame, 128 KiB reserved pools and pinned old frames, not only 192/224 bytes. Replace nonempty-root guards only together with full field reader/writer/integrity/retirement support. Evidence: `20261019-r3-native-root/`.
 
 An already-open handle authenticates the descriptor when acquiring a new metadata snapshot too; failure releases that new pin instead of allowing subsequent snapshots to continue through ROW after open-time validation. Descriptor reads use the file view's construction-time database ID, avoiding a race with writer rebinding of the mutable routing Superblock. The fixed charged PROFILE registry records `native_graph.read.metadata_decodes.hybrid_descriptor` (current-thread cumulative decode attempts). Resource caps are unchanged; registry/staging bounds derive from actual entry count.
+
+The 4096-membership ceiling applies to one explicit conversion plan, not the lifetime capacity of a converted group. The group header records the actual count in its declared u32 field; ordinary writes must retain existing dynamic-property support and checked count arithmetic. Conversion/reverse-conversion still refuses work above its bounded plan, rather than truncating, silently splitting publication, or treating 4096 as a permanent schema limit.
